{"id":660,"date":"2020-10-30T13:21:09","date_gmt":"2020-10-30T20:21:09","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=660"},"modified":"2024-08-30T09:39:13","modified_gmt":"2024-08-30T16:39:13","slug":"advanced-variables","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/advanced-variables\/","title":{"rendered":"2C: Advanced Variables"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214024729b\" name=\"6a2214024729b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting Started<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Make sure that your CoDrone Mini is paired before starting the lesson! If you forgot how to do this, make sure to review how to pair to Blockly <a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/cdmb-pairing\/\">here<\/a>.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221402473b1\" name=\"6a221402473b1\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a variable again?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Quick review: Variables are a way to store information in a program, and they\u2019re used most often to store values. Variables are great if you have a lot of values in your program since you only need to change the variable\u2019s value at the top of the program.\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214024740e\" name=\"6a2214024740e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Multiple variables<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Using multiple variables in one program might sound intimidating, but we promise it\u2019s not! The first step is to create variables for each value you think you need. For example, if you know you will always have your drone flying for 1 second at 50% power, you can create one variable for time and another for power. You can then set their values at the top of the program, so time\u2019s value would be 1 and power\u2019s variable would be 50.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;661&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try it out now! Set variables for your drone\u2019s time and power, and then use them to have your drone fly forward, yaw to the right, fly forward again, and land. After testing, try changing each variable\u2019s value to see what happens. <\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;662&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140247478\" name=\"6a22140247478\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Changing variables to a negative number<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You might be looking at your program and thinking, \u201cOK Robolink, that\u2019s great, but what if I want to fly backward or to the left? I need a negative number!\u201d There are two ways you can do that:<\/span><\/p>\n<ol>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can change the value at the top to a negative number. For example, instead of 50 for power, you can have -50 instead. This will change the value for the entire program.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In any instance where you need to use a negative number, you can use an equation block to multiply your variable by -1. This will help when you want to change some of your variables to a negative value but not all.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;663&#8243; img_size=&#8221;medium&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Change two of your power variables in your last program to -50. How is the flight pattern different?<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;664&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221402474ca\" name=\"6a221402474ca\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Variable math<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can also create multiple variables, set their values to integers, and use them throughout your program with the help of math equations. Your math teacher will be so excited to hear this!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Start out by creating four variables and assigning values to each of them. We used 1, 2, 5, and 10, but you can use whatever you like. Next, use them in equations in place of values or variables to have your drone roll to the right for 1 second at 50% power and throttle up for 2 seconds, also for 50% power! Again, you will need the flight variables blocks for this.\u00a0<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;668&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140247518\" name=\"6a22140247518\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Random integer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Buried deep in the math menu is the random integer block. When this block is used, it will generate a random number from the range that you set. However, this doesn\u2019t mean too much if you don\u2019t know what that number is! To see the number, attach the gray print block from the bottom of the logic menu to the random integer block. Generate three random numbers!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8886&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Check the console to see the output.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8887&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2214024755f\" name=\"6a2214024755f\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Storing a random integer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">A random integer can be stored in a variable! To do this, create a variable with a name you\u2019ll remember (ours is x), and then drag the set variable to block to your workspace. Next, attach the random integer block and make sure the values are set to what you want. To see what the selected random integer is when you run the code, attach a print block to the bottom of your code, and then attach a get variable block to that.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;675&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221402475a3\" name=\"6a221402475a3\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Adding to a flight program<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">What you can do now is add this to a program that will have your drone fly in a certain direction depending on the integer! In this program, there are three variables set to different random variables. The range is from -100 to 100 because power can be a positive or negative value. Run the code and guess what the values were! Hint: Add some print statements for each value to see if you were right.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;676&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22140247611\" name=\"6a22140247611\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Variable Square<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Program your drone to fly in a square, but there is a catch: you have to do it all with variables!\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rules:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You cannot use any numbers in your program except for setting up your variables at the top of your program.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">If you need to change the value of a variable, you can do it at the top of the program, use the change by block in the variables menu, or use a math equation.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Hints:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">One of the variables should be 0 for when you reset your flight directions.\u00a0<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Another should be -1 for when you need to go left, backward, or down.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 Getting Started [vc_column_text]Make sure that your CoDrone Mini is paired before starting the lesson! If you forgot how to do this, make sure to review how to&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/advanced-variables\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1671,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":true,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":407,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1814,1817,1763,1777,1737,1793,626,1533,1438,853,144,167,1392,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2127,2108,2125,2123,2122,2126,2117],"class_list":["post-660","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced-variables","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-fundamentals","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-senior","lesson-tag-variables","module-senior","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/660","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":9,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/660\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":8888,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/660\/revisions\/8888"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1671"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=660"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=660"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=660"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}